# `bridge::weighted_joint_loglik`

The weighted sum of a returns log-likelihood and an options log-likelihood.

## Signature

```cpp
double bridge::weighted_joint_loglik(double returns_loglik, double options_loglik, const bridge::JointLikelihoodWeights &weights = {});
```

## What It Does

`w_r · returns_loglik + w_o · options_loglik`, the objective to maximise when estimating pricing parameters from returns and option prices together. With the default weights it is the plain sum. See [`JointLikelihoodWeights`](JointLikelihoodWeights.md) for choosing the weights.

## Parameters

`double returns_loglik`
: Log-likelihood of the returns.

`double options_loglik`
: Log-likelihood of the option prices.

`const bridge::JointLikelihoodWeights &weights`
: The two weights.

## Returns

The weighted sum.

## Errors

- A negative or non-finite weight throws (`pluss::Error`).

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The default weights: a plain sum.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace ts = pluss::subsp::stats::ts;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(2));
  out.writeln(fin::bridge::weighted_joint_loglik(-1500.0, -120.0));
}
```

### Example 2

Weighted: the options count ten times as much.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace ts = pluss::subsp::stats::ts;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(2));
  out.writeln(fin::bridge::weighted_joint_loglik(-1500.0, -120.0, {.returns_weight = 1.0, .options_weight = 10.0}));
}
```

### Example 3

The joint objective from real pieces: the returns log-likelihood of a fitted model plus a Gaussian log-likelihood of option-price errors.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <cmath>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace ts = pluss::subsp::stats::ts;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(3));
  ts::GjrFit truth;
  truth.mean = 0.04; truth.omega = 0.03; truth.arch = {0.03}; truth.asym = {0.12}; truth.garch = {0.88};
  const Series returns = truth.simulate(1500, pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64), 11);
  const auto garch = ts::Garch::fit_mle(returns, {}, pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64));
  const auto gjr = ts::Gjr::fit_mle(returns, {}, pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64));
  const auto cfg = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);

  const double returns_loglik = fin::bridge::returns_metrics(returns, garch, cfg).loglik;
  const double errors[] = {0.05, -0.02, 0.01};
  double options_loglik = 0.0;
  for (const double e : errors) options_loglik += -0.5 * (e * e / 0.0025 + std::log(2.0 * M_PI * 0.0025));
  out.writeln("returns", returns_loglik, ", options", options_loglik, ", joint", fin::bridge::weighted_joint_loglik(returns_loglik, options_loglik, {.returns_weight = 1.0 / 1500.0, .options_weight = 1.0 / 3.0}));
}
```

### Example 4

Negative weights are refused.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <string>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace ts = pluss::subsp::stats::ts;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  try {
    (void)(fin::bridge::weighted_joint_loglik(-1.0, -1.0, {.returns_weight = -1.0}));
  } catch (const std::exception &e) {
    const std::string text(e.what());
    out.writeln(text.substr(0, text.find('\n')));
  }
}
```

